WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Secure input handling in C is about making the program explicit where C is permissive.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.500
The language gives you direct control over memory, pointers, buffers, integer types, and parsing behavior.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
That control is powerful, but it means the program must define and enforce its own boundaries.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:26.780
The safest code treats every external source as untrusted, whether the bytes arrive from a terminal, an argument, a file, a socket, a device, or another process.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.720
The strongest pattern is validation before use.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.780
Check read results before parsing.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.680
Check lengths before copying.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.420
Check numeric ranges before narrowing, allocating, indexing, or moving pointers.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.040
Check path policy before opening files.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.700
Check structured and binary fields before trusting counts, offsets, or embedded sizes.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:57.600
Preserve enough context to make those checks meaningful: buffer capacity, current length, expected format, allowed range, and actual data available.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.700
Good C input handling is also predictable under failure.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:07.560
Reject malformed, oversized, incomplete, inconsistent, or hostile input cleanly.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.780
Keep cleanup paths reliable.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.720
Avoid leaking sensitive data through diagnostics.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.340
Test the edge cases and the ugly cases, not just the happy path.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:23.480
When secure input handling becomes a routine engineering habit, C programs become easier to reason about.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.860
They do not rely on hope, friendly input, or accidental behavior.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.120
They rely on verified facts and deliberate boundaries.
